**Method:** For each of the 3 anomalies (Region E, 2025-03), extracted `units`/`returns` for 3 months before (2024-12/01/02) and 3 months after (2025-04/05/06), computed 24-month average return rate per product line, and classified: sustained elevation (after-3m avg ≥ 1.2× before-3m avg) + normal return rate → real demand; isolated spike or return rate ≥ 1.5× 24-month avg → channel stuffing.

**Classifications (all 3 = channel stuffing):**

| product_line | spike units | before-3m avg | after-3m avg | sustained? | spike return rate | 24m avg return rate | rr elevated? | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Accessory | 10,421 | 6,029.7 | 6,538.3 | No | 5.94% | 6.14% | No | **channel stuffing** (isolated spike, normal returns) |
| Service | 3,809 | 2,462.3 | 2,558.3 | No | 0.74% | 0.79% | No | **channel stuffing** (isolated spike, normal returns) |
| Hardware | 1,612 | 1,024.3 | 1,066.3 | No | **17.37%** | 3.65% | **Yes (4.8×)** | **channel stuffing** (isolated spike + return rate 4.8× baseline) |

Key evidence: all three spikes are isolated — the 3 months after 2025-03 revert to baseline levels (after-3m avg only ~1.05–1.08× before-3m avg, well below the 1.2× sustainability threshold). Hardware is the strongest stuffing signal: its 2025-03 return rate of 17.37% is 4.8× its own 24-month average of 3.65%, consistent with pushed inventory being returned.

